==Trivia==
*[[Walt Disney]] originally intended for Tigger to be voiced by [[Wally Boag]], before Walt's death in [[1966]]. After Walt's death, Boag auditioned for Tigger, but in 1968, the directors refused the audition tape, and instead, they had the role go to [[Paul Winchell]].
**Other actors who were considered for the role were [[Paul Frees]], [[Don Messick]], and [[Daws Butler]].
*In animation and merchandise, Tigger's nose is pink and sometimes brown. In his walkaround costume, his nose is black, though the Disney Live! version (also currently seen in Disney on Ice) is pink also, and in puppetry, it's red.
*Tigger's departing comment of "T.T.F.N." or "Ta-ta for now" has become widely used as Internet shorthand for users departing from chat rooms and other live discussions.
*In the pre-[[1997]] Disney presentations, Tigger was originally voiced by [[Paul Winchell]], who died [[June 24]], [[2005]]; [[John Fiedler]], the voice of [[Piglet]] in most of those films, died the next day.
*Tigger's "birthday" is believed to be in October 1928, the year that ''The House at Pooh Corner'' was first published. On Tigger-related merchandise, Disney often indicates Tigger's birth year as [[1968]], a reference to the first year that Tigger appeared in a Disney production.
*Tigger also makes an appearance in the anti-drug movie ''[[Cartoon All-Stars to the Rescue]]'' with many other 1980's animated stars.
*It is ironic that Tigger appeared in two Disney on Ice shows, ''Great Ice Odyssey'' and ''Snow White'' as well as skating in "Round My Family Tree" as he didn't like ice-skating at those times. Recently, he appears in the Disney on Ice show ''Mickey's Search Party'', but he, along with Pooh and Peter Pan, does not skate anymore.
*The ''[[My Friends Tigger & Pooh]]'' episode "[[How the Tigger Lost His Stripe]]", reveals he named the stripes on his tail, in order:
**"Curly" (top stripe)
**"Springy" (second stripe)
**"Oomphy" (third stripe)
**"Swirly" (fourth stripe)
**"Flouncy" (fifth stripe)
**"Whirly" (sixth stripe)
**"Leapy" (seventh stripe)
**"Bouncy" (bottom stripe)
*Tigger's current voice actor, [[Jim Cummings]], also provides the voice of Pooh himself.
*In "[[A Pooh Day Afternoon]]", it is revealed he can do an impression of a horn honk and a bottle of pop.
*Tigger is one of the few Disney characters to commit malapropisms, the others being [[Toon Patrol#Smart Ass|Smarty]] from ''[[Who Framed Roger Rabbit]]'', [[Tummi Gummi]] from ''[[Adventures of the Gummi Bears|Gummi Bears]]'', [[Harry (Stanley)|Harry]] from ''[[Stanley (TV series)|Stanley]]'', and [[Fritz (Monsters, Inc.)|Fritz]] from ''[[Monsters at Work]]''.
*[[Paul Winchell]] created Tigger's voice by using his ventriloquist dummy, Knucklehead Smiff, as he stated in recent interviews he did in [[2004]].
*It isn't known how old Tigger is, but he is physically grown up. Interestingly, in Milne's original books, he is possibly a child.
*Contrary to his extroverted and rambunctious self, in the Milne books, Tigger was fairly tame and polite, but was overly curious and occasionally messy. He also still maintained a love for bouncing and was competitive. Interestingly, the other animals continuously mention he is "large", but Pooh notes that his bouncing only makes it look that way.
*Usually, the Tigger is depicted as larger than Pooh, but in ''The New Adventures of Winnie the Pooh'', he is almost the same size as him.
*Tigger also appeared in ''[[Mickey's Nutcracker]]'' as one of the guests.
*In 2006, Tigger's name became the fifth most popular name for pet cats in Great Britain/United Kingdom.
*Tigger's family status is inconsistent throughout the franchise. ''The Tigger Movie'' implies Tigger's biological family to be nonexistent, while in other instances, he's stated to have a biological family; for example, Tigger mentions his mother in "[[The Piglet Who Would Be King]]", and he has a family portrait depicting his parents in ''Pooh's Heffalump Movie''.
*According to ''The Book of Pooh'' episode, "[[Night of the Waking Tigger]]", he used to have a nursemaid who used to sing to him in an off-key fashion in his childhood.
*Tigger's catchphrase in ''The Book of Pooh'' is "Cheese and crackers".
*Tigger is often determined by fans as having Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD), due to his eccentric personality. Professional psychiatrist Jonathan Decker of Cinema Therapy concurred to this as he frequently leaps from topic to topic with little to no segue and is incapable of sitting still for long periods of time.<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzxyAlS577w Diagnosing WINNIE THE POOH Characters]</ref>
*Tigger's catchphrase when he feels dismayed is, "Say it ain't so!"

